      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Middle East, Asia, and Africa did contribute to the development of various sciences and philosophies, but their impact was largely limited to their own regions and cultures. The ancient Mesopotamians, for example, made significant contributions to mathematics and astronomy, but their ideas were largely absorbed by the Greeks. The Indian subcontinent's contributions to mathematics, medicine, and philosophy, such as the works of Aryabhata and Charaka, were influential in the Islamic world, but their impact on Western civilization was largely indirect. The Islamic Golden Age did see significant advances in science, mathematics, and philosophy, but these achievements were largely confined to the Middle East and North Africa. The African continent, on the other hand, was largely isolated from the rest of the world until the 15th century, and its contributions to modern science and law were largely limited to the period of colonialism.",
